WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:05.820
Agent Curren was your typical SCP Foundation
field worker. While sometimes she spent her

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:10.680
days infiltrating the hideaways of dangerous
Groups of Interest such as the Chaos Insurgency

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:15.850
or the Serpent’s Hand, or protecting a small
town from a monstrous anomaly set on devouring

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:21.650
each and every inhabitant, the vast majority
of her work was routine. And with that mundane

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:27.320
workload came boredom. Filing papers, filling
out reports, and running to get her supervisor's

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:32.840
coffee were just as much a part of her job
as keeping the anomalous secured, contained,

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:37.730
and protected from the outside world. As a
result, Agent Curren had a lot of time on

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:43.760
her hands, and one way she enjoyed filling
it was with a good book. She loved to read,

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.550
sometimes even more than she loved her real
job. Sometimes she thought about taking a

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:52.910
position observing the Wanderer’s Library,
where she could have a multiversal bibliography

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:58.079
to pour over and more free time than she’d
know what to do with. It makes sense, if you

00:00:58.079 --> 00:01:04.129
had to face dangers as frequently as an agent
of the SCP Foundation does, which is usually

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:10.539
on a weekly basis, then you’d want an escape
from real life too. For Agent Curren, that’s

00:01:10.539 --> 00:01:15.119
what reading was, and she liked being able
to say she actually read all those literary

00:01:15.119 --> 00:01:18.909
classics that people pretend to have read
to sound smart.

00:01:18.909 --> 00:01:23.740
One aspect of Agent Curren’s job was inspecting
areas around the location she was stationed

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:29.390
at for potential anomalous activity. This
included keeping up with local news, checking

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:34.520
second hand stores for anomalous objects that
might have unknowingly gone up for sale, or

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:39.229
simply hanging around town and waiting for
something interesting to happen. Most of the

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:44.780
time, it didn’t. Agent Curren was located
in a medium sized Texas town, where the most

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:50.241
exciting thing that happened on any given
day was a sale at the local supermarket. This

00:01:50.241 --> 00:01:55.450
left her with a lot of reading time, and thankfully,
the town had a bookstore. It was a second

00:01:55.450 --> 00:02:01.279
hand shop, selling used books and old newspapers.
For someone who’d read all the mainstream

00:02:01.279 --> 00:02:07.210
staples of literature, this place was a treasure
trove of interesting finds. Books she’d

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:11.660
never heard of, books she wasn’t sure were
ever printed on a high enough run. There were

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:16.210
some truly valuable and rare finds in this
shop, and Agent Curren made it a point to

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:21.180
visit the store whenever she had the opportunity
to. The most difficult part of her daily patrol

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:26.090
was not spending all of it inside the shop,
pouring over each and every new and interesting

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:31.970
book she came across. But one day, she found
something new. Something that intertwined

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:37.500
her love of literature with her career focus
in containing the supernatural. She stumbled

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.420
upon SCP-423.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:45.160
While walking down the aisle of stuffed bookshelves,
she found a copy of Mark Twain’s Tom Sawyer

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:50.270
sitting on the floor, piled atop a stack of
books, and almost inviting her to pick it

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:55.220
up. Tom Sawyer was one of the first books
she read on her own as a kid, and Agent Curren

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:59.770
had a fondness for the novel ever since. She
picked the book up and thumbed through a few

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:05.910
pages out of habit, not expecting to see anything
she hadn’t. And at first, she didn’t.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:11.170
But one single sentence caught her eye, in
which Tom was described as talking to another

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:16.790
child by the name of Fred. Now, Agent Curren
was a well-read bibliophile, and she knew

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:22.270
Twain’s masterpiece like the back of her
hand. There was certainly no Fred in Tom Sawyer.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:27.370
Intrigued, she read on. The following pages
depicted an extended conversation between

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:32.320
Tom Sawyer and this mysterious Fred, regarding
the types of snakes that live in the woods

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:38.340
that surround the quiet town of St. Petersburg,
Missouri. Agent Curren was taken back completely.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.960
Was this a part of the book she had forgotten
about? Sensing a potential oddity, she decided

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.380
to purchase the book, and compare it to the
various copies in her expansive, personal

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:51.790
collection. While she didn’t find any new
books to read on her trip to the store that

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:57.800
day, she did find something to occupy her
mind, and for Agent Curren, that was enough

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:00.230
to make the quiet days pass.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:05.709
When she returned back to the Foundation facility
she was stationed at, located in Sector 28,

00:04:05.709 --> 00:04:09.819
Agent Curren immediately began cross referencing
the copy of Tom Sawyer with other copies that

00:04:09.819 --> 00:04:14.300
were available to her. She rented out as many
as she could from the public libraries in

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:19.680
the area, and even used the Foundation’s
printed works archive. She found as many printings

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.021
as possible, even multiple of the same printing
that the copy she had bought from the book

00:04:24.021 --> 00:04:29.360
store was supposedly from. But when she began
her research, she found that every copy of

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.729
Tom Sawyer was missing the conversation about
snakes. There were no characters by the name

00:04:34.729 --> 00:04:40.319
of Fred in any printing of the books. She
read the entirety of the copy that she purchased

00:04:40.319 --> 00:04:44.800
from the store, and on top of the strange
conversation, she also found several other

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:50.270
mentions of this Fred character, who was apparently
a boy that Tom Sawyer went to school with.

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:55.039
Fred was an incredibly minor character, but
still interacted with the protagonists on

00:04:55.039 --> 00:04:59.780
occasion, and was even mentioned a few times
by Tom and his other friend Huck while he

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:04.110
wasn’t present. For someone with as little
of a role as Fred, the characters sure seemed

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:09.770
to pay him a lot of attention. Though it was
definitely weird, Agent Curren didn’t find

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:14.090
anything particularly anomalous about the
book, at least at first. It could’ve been

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:18.669
a practical joke between people with a very
strange sense of humor, or simply the work

00:05:18.669 --> 00:05:23.430
of someone wanting to see if they could get
away with editing a copy of a literary masterpiece

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:27.849
and selling it in hopes that some poor sucker
would discover it and set off a craze about

00:05:27.849 --> 00:05:33.789
a misprinting. Whatever the answer was, it
was decidedly ordinary, and that didn’t

00:05:33.789 --> 00:05:38.860
sit well with Agent Curren. She wanted answers,
but she knew deep down that getting them was

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:44.819
going to be a tall order. But during her research,
she discovered something that would earn the

00:05:44.819 --> 00:05:48.539
object an SCP classification.

00:05:48.539 --> 00:05:52.570
One day, while sitting at her computer terminal
and pretending to work, she was reading a

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:57.749
copy of Herman Melville’s Moby Dick. Less
than a meter away sat the strange copy of

00:05:57.749 --> 00:06:03.449
Tom Sawyer she had bought at the bookstore
a week earlier. Before her eyes, the words

00:06:03.449 --> 00:06:09.729
on the pages of Moby Dick started to disappear
or alter themselves into new ones. The book

00:06:09.729 --> 00:06:15.259
felt heavier, and Agent Curren swore she felt
new pages being added to it. When all the

00:06:15.259 --> 00:06:20.409
rapid alterations slowed down, she took another
look at the pages of Moby Dick, and her jaw

00:06:20.409 --> 00:06:25.629
nearly dropped to the floor in shock at what
she was reading. The book now described a

00:06:25.629 --> 00:06:31.289
sailor by the name of Fred, stationed on the
Pequod, the ship that was hunting the giant

00:06:31.289 --> 00:06:36.240
sperm whale given the name Moby-Dick. The
book once again did not describe Fred as doing

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:41.400
anything particularly noteworthy. He was in
the background of the story, said some dialogue

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:46.249
to Captain Ahab about the ship’s upkeep,
and was mostly regulated to standing around

00:06:46.249 --> 00:06:50.860
and watching, even during the scenes that
described whaling. By now, Agent Curren was

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:56.330
positive the book wasn’t just a strange
misprint, it was an anomalous object, just

00:06:56.330 --> 00:07:01.349
as she originally suspected. She reported
the find to her supervisors, and a research

00:07:01.349 --> 00:07:04.780
team was brought in shortly after to analyze
the book.

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:08.990
First, the team observed the anomaly’s method
of transmission, which was the placement of

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:14.219
a character named Fred, who took the form
of an unimportant, minor character in the

00:07:14.219 --> 00:07:18.650
story, between book to book. The anomaly was
able to move between narratives if they were

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:23.860
placed within a one meter radius of one another
for more than 3 minutes, like when it jumped

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:28.490
from the copy of Tom Sawyer, to a copy of
Moby Dick, and later, when the research team

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:34.610
initially tested it, to a copy of another
book. One the anomalous effects were established,

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:41.800
the anomaly was given the designation of SCP-423,
nicknamed the “Self Inserting Character”.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:47.899
SCP-423, as we just described, does not have
a physical form. It’s the character Fred,

00:07:47.899 --> 00:07:53.009
who is always an incredibly minor character
with an overall lack of narrative importance.

00:07:53.009 --> 00:07:57.610
Fred is usually not physically described in
the story, but when he is, he appears as an

00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:02.150
adult male of average height and middle age,
but the Foundation quickly discovered that

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:07.740
this could change. In narratives focusing
primarily around children, such as Tom Sawyer,

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:12.499
Fred was a child. In others that featured
non-human characters, such as George Orwell’s

00:08:12.499 --> 00:08:18.719
Animal Farm, Fred was an animal. Whatever
the story called for, SCP-423 fit the constraints

00:08:18.719 --> 00:08:24.280
of the narrative. Other characters, when they
address or acknowledge SCP-423, do not see

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:30.360
its inclusion as odd or unusual, and the story
proceeds as usual with minor alterations to

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:31.360
the text.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:36.380
The Foundation discovered that SCP-423, if
transferred to a journal, has the capability

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:41.160
to communicate via writing back and forth
between a researcher and the anomaly. This

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:47.030
allowed the Foundation to discern SCP-423’s
nature easier, and figure out how exactly

00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:51.940
the anomaly works. But more on that later.
From the Foundation’s communications with

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:57.840
SCP-423, they found that it prefers fictional
narratives, but testing proved that it can

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:04.380
enter itself into any written work that features
a narrative. That means biographies, anecdotes,

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:09.540
and even the Foundation’s very own research
notes. As long as the prerequisite requirements

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:15.490
for a narrative are present, such as names,
events, places, and the bare skeleton of a

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:19.200
story, SCP-423 can supplant itself into it.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:24.770
The process is also confined to physical,
textual narratives. Purely visual or electronic

00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:30.970
mediums such as television shows, video games,
and computers are all off limits for SCP-423.

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:37.930
But testing has shown that SCP-423 is capable
of entering graphic novels and comic books,

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:42.680
though when the text becomes altered, it will
only affect dialogue and text boxes. Paneling

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:47.750
and art are unable to be alerted, and will
remain the same, but the textual elements

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:54.730
around the panels will change themselves to
make way for SCP-423’s inclusion. When SCP-423

00:09:54.730 --> 00:10:00.050
is placed before a narrative it has previously
entered, the effect will still take place,

00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:04.810
but this time the narrative will become slightly
altered once more, differing from the previous

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:12.050
time SCP-423 entered the narrative. While
this is possible, communication with SCP-423

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:16.560
has shown that it much prefers to enter narratives
it has not previously seen.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:22.500
As testing with SCP-423 is limited to introducing
it to new narratives, the Foundation hasn’t

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:28.261
been able to answer a lot of questions regarding
SCP-423’s capabilities. Does it eat? Does

00:10:28.261 --> 00:10:34.440
it sleep? What would happen if it died, and
can it? While the answers to these are unknown,

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:40.260
SCP-423 has shown it possesses a high degree
of narrative awareness and tends to keep itself

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:47.250
out of trouble or situations that would result
in death. At most, SCP-423 has been lightly

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:52.630
injured in some narratives. But as soon as
it enters a new book, the injuries it harbored

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:56.260
in the previous one seem to vanish entirely.

00:10:56.260 --> 00:11:01.820
As we mentioned before, the Foundation can
communicate with SCP-423 through a journal.

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:07.480
When SCP-423 is introduced to the journal,
which has questions written inside beforehand

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:13.350
to coax the entity in. After transmission
has taken place, SCP-423’s answers to those

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:18.670
questions will appear underneath. Once the
entity is introduced to a new narrative, however,

00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:24.500
the answers will disappear entirely, meaning
the Foundation has to record them before conducting

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:27.840
any further testing with SCP-423.

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:34.170
It is not believed that SCP-423 is a malicious
entity, as Fred has been incredibly cooperative

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:38.970
with the Foundation, answering questions and
hopping from narrative to narrative during

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:44.840
testing. In her few attempts to talk with
the entity, Agent Curren and SCP-423 got along

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:49.970
well, and spoke about various books. Though
Curren was instructed to not make any promises

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:56.630
that the Foundation could introduce SCP-423
to any book it wanted. Mainly, SCP-423 has

00:11:56.630 --> 00:12:01.970
focused its efforts on asking the Foundation
for narratives with lots of background characters,

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:06.950
specifically because this makes it easier
to blend in and, in Fred’s words, “watch

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:12.020
the good stuff”. Largely, the Foundation
has complied with these requests, under the

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:18.310
condition that if at any point SCP-423 becomes
uncooperative, it will be confined inside

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:22.960
the journal for an extended period of time
until it starts to listen again.

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:28.880
While SCP-423 isn’t dangerous in of itself,
Foundation personnel handling it still have

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:34.290
to keep an eye out. If they’re taking written
notes, for whatever reason, within SCP-423’s

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:40.170
presence, the entity can transfer itself to
them. As a result, all research done regarding

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:46.440
SCP-423 must be electronically recorded. Thankfully,
the inclusion of a Guest Researcher Fred in

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:53.200
any seasoned reasearcher’s SCP file is going
to raise some eyebrows, so possible SCP-423

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:59.380
intrusions are easy enough to detect. SCP-423’s
containment procedures are also similarly

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:05.520
lax, keeping the anomaly inside a plain, marked
journal with the label ‘423’.

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:10.980
Due to the anomaly’s relatively passive
nature, the Foundation has tested SCP-423

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:15.760
with a plethora of narratives, just to see
how it might alter them and if those alterations

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:20.510
could garner any new information about the
anomaly. Let’s take a look at some of them.

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:26.010
SCP-423’s experiments were authorized by
the Overseer Council and conducted by the

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:32.800
anomaly’s research head, the esteemed Dr.
Everett Mann. In all tests, SCP-423 was introduced

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:38.010
to an outside narrative, and after alterations
took place, the text was read by Foundation

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:41.320
personnel and recorded in the testing log.

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:47.880
First up, SCP-423 was introduced to Tolkien’s
“The Hobbit”. A 14th dwarf named Feredor

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:52.250
was present in Gandalf’s party of 13 on
their quest to reclaim the dwarves' mountain

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:57.880
home from the dragon Smaug. There were small
changes to the text, such as a lack of a ‘lucky

00:13:57.880 --> 00:14:01.910
number’ being referenced, and the death
of the character Oin in the final portion

00:14:01.910 --> 00:14:07.770
of the novel. Feredor survives the journey.
Dr. Mann had this to say about the test:

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:13.780
"SCP-423's role is larger in this work, allowing
a better look at its effects. The dialog written

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:18.590
for the character is similar to that written
for the other dwarves. Other differences in

00:14:18.590 --> 00:14:22.380
text match Tolkien's writing style as used
in the rest of the book."

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:28.140
Another test introduced SCP-423 to J. K. Rowling’s
Harry Potter and the Half Blood Prince, but

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:33.300
to little effect. A wizard named Fred appears,
but very few changes in the actual story were

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:40.940
noted, save for a scene in which SCP-423 is
confused for an existing character in the

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:42.440
series, Fred Weasely. Dr. Mann noted:

00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:46.220
"SCP-423 displayed paranormal abilities in
the narrative, though nothing out of note

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:51.450
for the fictional universe. However, when
returned to the journal, SCP-423 said that

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:55.800
it couldn't duplicate them outside of that
particular narrative universe."

00:14:55.800 --> 00:15:02.000
When SCP-423 was introduced to James Joyce’s
rambling, incoherent masterpiece, Ulysses,

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:06.300
which is composed of what might as well be
gibberish, the entity immediately returned

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:11.850
to its journal, where it wrote out "Ow, ow,
bad idea." Dr. Mann noted:

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:17.020
“SCP-423 dislikes the book, and considers
it a potential punishment if he misbehaves.”

00:15:17.020 --> 00:15:22.790
In the next text, SCP-423 was introduced to
a lyrics sheet for the classical piece “Battle

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:27.010
Hymn of the Republic”, written by Julia
Ward Howe. If you don’t know it, look it

00:15:27.010 --> 00:15:33.320
up after this video, because you’ve definitely
heard it before. Once inside, SCP-423 altered

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:39.030
the lyrics. "I have seen Him in the watch-fires
of a hundred circling camps" and "I can read

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:44.490
His righteous sentence by the dim and flaring
lamps" were changed to "Fred has seen Him

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:49.779
in the watch-fires of a hundred circling camps"
and "Fred can read His righteous sentence

00:15:49.779 --> 00:15:55.640
by the dim and flaring lamps". Upon return,
SCP-423 stated that it found the experience

00:15:55.640 --> 00:15:59.090
interesting, but ultimately did not want to
do it again.

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:03.460
When introduced to the nursery rhyme Mary
Had a Little Lamb, it became Freddy Had a

00:16:03.460 --> 00:16:10.830
Little Lamb. A book of 365 Haiku was altered
to include lines such as "Fred watched silently",

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:18.950
or "Fred is also here", and "Only Fred remains".
A phone book noted no change, and no reaction

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:24.720
from SCP-423. An Aesop fable written on a
metal surface with magnetic plastic letters

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:30.650
saw new letters appear from the same material
and composition to include a mention of SCP-423.

00:16:30.650 --> 00:16:37.310
An english braille edition of Homer’s Odyssey
noted no changes, though SCP-423 commented

00:16:37.310 --> 00:16:42.290
that the experience was more boring than anything
else. When introduced to a hardcopy of the

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:48.650
SCP-423 experiment log, no changes were noted
aside from Fred being described as “ruggedly

00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:51.120
handsome” in several sections.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:56.530
When researchers attempted to introduce SCP-423
to The Kugelmass Episode, a story written

00:16:56.530 --> 00:17:01.160
by Woody Allen about a man who is able to
travel into fiction, the Overseer Council

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:06.110
forbade the experiment from proceeding, unsure
of what could happen if two meta-aware characters

00:17:06.110 --> 00:17:10.920
made contact with one another.
In another SCP-423 experiment, the Foundation

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:17.010
gave Fred his very own story. Foundation Researcher
Torrez wrote a small novella, appropriately

00:17:17.010 --> 00:17:22.959
titled “Fred’s Story”, specifically
to test SCP-423’s capabilities as acting

00:17:22.959 --> 00:17:28.100
as a main character. Fred’s Story was set
in a fantasy kingdom, inhabited entirely by

00:17:28.100 --> 00:17:33.130
beautiful women. When the kingdom is attacked,
the queen performs a ritual to summon a hero

00:17:33.130 --> 00:17:38.300
from another dimension to save them. The hero
is never given a name, but it is mentioned

00:17:38.300 --> 00:17:42.270
that he is male and can transfer himself to
different works of fiction.

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:47.511
While SCP-423 was flattered that he had a
narrative all to himself, he didn’t change

00:17:47.511 --> 00:17:53.340
much in the story. The hero was still unnamed,
and the text was largely unaltered, save for

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:58.070
a single sentence mentioning the queen’s
handmaiden “Frederica”. After returning

00:17:58.070 --> 00:18:01.240
to the journal, SCP-423 had this to say:

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:06.430
"I appreciate the offer, but I just can't.
I never deserved to be in the spotlight."

00:18:06.430 --> 00:18:13.020
Now go check out “SCP-1025 - Encyclopedia
of Common Diseases” and “SCP-1230 - A

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:17.290
Hero is Born” for more anomalously literature-crazed
SCPs!

